{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791375052,"nonce":"+S94baIQC7mHpCBTO7+HOwOyc+4=","title":"Benchmark: explain fannkuch slowdown with identical emitted native code","message":"During HIR byte-range load reuse work (56e2dd1a0c47abdd59f04afa35655a922e6ff3719470b15d46cbce35233ad18c), a saved 5feb4e57ec30e464b0547cad10a2f0c357d64d75 baseline consistently runs standard-count fannkuch faster than rebuilt candidates. The gap survives disabling both new runtime HIR transforms in the same executable, and the disabled candidate emits identical native instruction sequences for all 77 traced functions after normalizing embedded host addresses.\n\nFinal NUC three-way control: LLVM disabled, interpreter-first/Cranelift tiering, affinity 0-7, five rotating processes per arm, harness count 50. Baseline mean 39.403 ms / wall 2032.657 ms. Candidate with ZYNTAX_DISABLE_LOAD_RANGES=1 and ZYNTAX_DISABLE_LATE_LOAD_CLEANUP=1: 41.884 / 2157.544 ms. Candidate with both enabled: 41.829 / 2154.893 ms. Thus the rebuilt executable is about 6% slower than the saved baseline, while the runtime transforms have no measurable additional cost here. Do not call this a resolved regression or a Python performance gain.\n\nThe flags do not undo build-time builtins snapshots. However, native comparison covers all 77 functions compiled in the three-iteration trace, including the OSR resume body and bound methods. The enabled candidate changes four frontend-generated bodies by removing jump blocks; disabled candidate has no native-code differences from baseline after address normalization. HIR-only block-removal attribution was not established: limiting late rewrites to functions with memory savings also failed to recover the between-build gap and was discarded.\n\nInvestigate the remaining execution path with a suitable profiler: host/runtime code and executable placement, interpreter activity not represented in native dumps, dispatch/OSR readiness, and scheduling/CPU effects remain hypotheses. On this NUC perf_event_paranoid is 4 and sudo -n perf requires interactive authentication, so hardware counters were unavailable; no system settings were changed. Shared benchmark lock was held; existing desktop/HUD processes remained running.\n\nArtifacts on NUC and Mac: /tmp/zyntax-load-ranges-v9-control-final.json; /tmp/zyntax-load-ranges-fannkuch-{baseline,v9off,v9on}-all.trace. NUC HIR dumps: /tmp/zyntax-load-ranges-fannkuch-{baseline,v9off,v9on}-hir. Driver: /tmp/zyntax-load-ranges-v9-control-final.py. Baseline binaries /tmp/{zypy,zylua}-known-bits-control, candidate /tmp/{zypy,zylua}-load-ranges.\n\nBinary SHA-256 values:\n/tmp/zylua-known-bits-control: 25426c8e94e25b11b2e64ff5320beca25b62f9f6dae7a2b225632d7319ef1c17\n/tmp/zylua-load-ranges: d2bf0af6b2da4d0bfd3c6328a82543dbeefc59ab8ba88647eb8f47f5243ce326\n/tmp/zypy-known-bits-control: 39b4f2c99a9a4f81e384ca0fd948439a899e5d5d103db3298bc16a2821c8bd60\n/tmp/zypy-load-ranges: 2e49eda0941d0ac35d437369ea6a4eb7a151eb522ce22b0b7c9eae95c48f09e3","files":null}]}